== Cognitive science Domain decision ==

Wave 4 introduces the controlled '''Cognitive science''' Domain because the corpus is explicitly interdisciplinary and cannot be accurately reduced to Philosophy, Science, Computer science, or a not-yet-approved Psychology Domain.

The implementation contract is:

<pre>
bitwiki-runtime-schema.json
        ↕ audited projection
Module:BITwiki/Data/Schema
        +
Category:Cognitive science
        +
Portal:Cognitive science
        +
Cognition as Domain exemplar
</pre>

Psychology remains a future independent Domain decision.

== Cognitive-bias normalization ==

The wave must not turn "bias" into a synonym for stupidity or irrationality.

A bias claim requires a comparison standard. Heuristics can be efficient and adaptive in some environments while still producing systematic error relative to a particular benchmark.

[[Selection bias]] is deliberately included as a contrast and uses Science/Mathematics domains because its mechanism is sampling/conditioning, not cognition.

== Priming normalization ==

Core/Technical pages separate robust experimental families such as repetition, perceptual, and semantic priming from broader social/behavioral priming claims whose replication record is more mixed.

No page may use priming as evidence that arbitrary subliminal cues reliably control complex behavior.

== Mental-control normalization ==

The classic white-bear literature motivates [[Ironic process theory]], but current evidence does not support the simplistic universal claim:

<pre>
try not to think X → X necessarily rebounds
</pre>

[[Thought suppression]] must describe immediate suppression, rebound, memory-suppression paradigms, cognitive load, and individual/context differences separately.

[[Ironic process theory]] is a specific historical/theoretical model whose predictions remain empirically testable rather than axiomatic.

== Public concept versus AI/BITwiki application ==

<pre>
working memory        ≠ LLM context window
attention             ≠ Transformer attention
memory                ≠ vector database
learning              ≠ machine learning
metacognition         ≠ chain-of-thought
cognitive load        ≠ token count
theory of mind        ≠ one LLM benchmark
distributed cognition ≠ multi-agent system
</pre>

AI applications may appear only as explicitly labeled applications or contrasts.

== Source roles ==

* Baddeley and working-memory reviews: working memory.
* Standard cognitive psychology/cognitive neuroscience references: perception, attention, memory, learning, reasoning.
* Flavell; Nelson & Narens: metacognition.
* Sweller and later instructional-psychology reviews: cognitive load.
* Tversky & Kahneman; Nickerson; Gigerenzer and judgment literature: heuristics/biases.
* Priming literature: repetition, perceptual, semantic, and cautious social/behavioral treatment.
* Epley/Waytz/Cacioppo and comparative-cognition literature: anthropomorphism.
* Developmental/social-cognition literature: theory of mind.
* Hutchins; Hollan/Hutchins/Kirsh: distributed cognition.
* Barsalou and 4E literature: embodied/grounded cognition.
* Wegner historical literature plus contemporary suppression reviews: thought suppression / ironic processes.
* Epidemiology/statistics/causal inference: selection bias.

== Reader linking rule ==

Core pages link to Core titles.

Simple pages prefer Simple variants where available.

Technical pages prefer Technical variants where available.

Simple/Technical surfaces are presentations of one Core semantic identity and do not duplicate <code>Knowledge object</code> or category identity.

